= Templates and categories =

Templates and categories solve different problems.

* '''Template''' = reusable/transcluded content, structure, or interface.
* '''Category''' = human-readable grouping and automatic index.
* '''Property''' = Semantic MediaWiki attribute or relationship.

Do not use one merely because another would require more thought.

== Template rules ==

A canonical V2 template should have:

# a clear purpose;
# documented parameters;
# one minimal usage example;
# explicit dependencies where relevant;
# source/provenance notes when inherited from V1;
# its own template category inside <code>&lt;noinclude&gt;</code>.

Use <code>&lt;includeonly&gt;</code> only for behavior/content intended to affect transcluding pages. Use <code>&lt;noinclude&gt;</code> for template documentation and categories that belong to the template itself.

Do not delete an unused V1 template merely because [[Special:UnusedTemplates]] lists it. It may be a compatibility surface, an abandoned experiment worth archiving, or a component whose callers were removed. Recover the body and usage history first.

== Category rules ==

A V2 category page should state, when useful:

* what the category groups;
* inclusion/exclusion criteria;
* parent category/categories;
* whether it is a domain, navigation group, operational/meta grouping, or another intentional class.

Category pages are indexes, not directories. A page may belong to multiple categories. Category hierarchy is a navigation graph, not the ontology.

Do not create a category solely to mirror every semantic property value.

